---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
import Features from '../components/Features.astro';
import Providers from '../components/Providers.astro';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <Hero />

  <section id="why" class="reveal ui-section">
    <div class="ui-section__inner">
      <div class="max-w-[760px] mx-auto w-full flex flex-col gap-7">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p class="m-0 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-lime">
            Why it exists
          </p>
          <h2 class="ui-section-title m-0">Why a second provider breaks your setup.</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="why-prose flex flex-col gap-5 text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-text-dim max-[640px]:text-[16px]">
          <p>
            Claude Code keeps one config directory: <code>~/.claude</code>. It holds
            settings, MCP servers, plugins, skills, and conversation history. That works
            while you use one provider. Add a second and the same files have to serve both
            setups. Change the base URL for GLM and your Anthropic sessions follow it. Swap
            in a DeepSeek key and the old key is gone. Copy the directory by hand and you
            are maintaining two setups that drift.
          </p>
          <p>
            claude-multi is a free, MIT-licensed CLI for people who switch providers during
            the day. It ships eight templates for six providers: GLM, MiniMax, DeepSeek,
            MiMo, Kimi, and Qwen. You might keep Claude on your main account, use GLM for a
            long refactor, and send cheap batch work to DeepSeek. Or you might test a model
            before moving a team onto it. The goal is to change models without rebuilding
            the setup around them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Isolation is literal. <code>claude-multi add glm</code> creates
            <code>~/.claude-glm/</code> with its own <code>settings.json</code>,
            credentials, sessions, and history. The <code>claude-glm</code> wrapper sets
            <code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code> to that directory, then runs the official
            <code>claude</code> binary. Your normal <code>~/.claude</code> setup is left as
            it is. Plugins and skills can be symlinked from your primary install, so you
            install them once. MCP servers remain entries in the instance's
            <code>settings.json</code>. The central registry at
            <code>~/.claude-multi/</code> tracks instances, health, and backups. It does not
            store each instance's settings.
          </p>
          <p>
            Install with <code>npm install -g claude-multi</code>, or use bun, pnpm, or
            Deno. Run <code>claude-multi</code> for the menu, or
            <code>claude-multi add glm</code> to create an instance and paste a key. The
            <a href="/docs/">setup guide</a> covers the rest, the
            <a href="/providers/">provider pages</a> list endpoints and model mappings, the
            <a href="/blog/">blog</a> has notes on model choice, and the
            <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> answers common questions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Install />
  <Features />
  <Providers />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .why-prose code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .why-prose a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .why-prose a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
